solution.p:
To find the projection, divide the dot product by the magnitude of \vec{AB}


    \|\proj_{\vec{AB}}\vec{T}\| \amp = \frac {\vec{T} \cdot \vec{AB}}{AB} = \frac{-33.32\ \textrm{N} \cancel{\textrm{m}}}{0.4\ \cancel{\textrm{m}}}


    \amp = \N{-83.30}

First, find the unit vector
\widehat{\vec{AB}} = \frac{\vec{AB}}{AB}= \frac{\m{\langle 0.3464, -0.2, 0 \rangle}}{\m{0.4}} = \langle 0.866, -0.5, 0 \rangle Or, since \vec{AB} is in the xy plane with its direction defined by the \alpha = \ang{30}, the unit vector \widehat{\vec{AB}} is found \widehat{\vec{AB}} = \langle \cos \ang{30}, - \sin \ang{30}, 0 \rangle = \langle 0.866, -0.5, 0 \rangle.
Then find the projection


    \|\proj_{\vec{AB}}\vec{T}\| \amp = \vec{T} \cdot \widehat{\vec{AB}}


    \amp = \left( \N{-50} \right) \left( 0.866 \right) + \left( \N{80} \right) \left( -0.5 \right) + \left( \N{40} \right) \left( 0 \right)


    \amp = \N{-83.30}

We want to find the part of \vec{T} perpendicular to \vec{AB}, so we can rearrange the equation to find:


    \vec{T} \perp \vec{AB} \amp = \vec{T}-\proj_{\vec{AB}}\vec{T}


    \amp = \langle -50, 80, 40 \rangle - \langle -72.14, 41.65, 0 \rangle


    \amp = \N{\langle 22.14, 38.35, 40 \rangle}

First, establish a coordinate system with the origin P and with the x axis aligned with \vec{A}, then find the rectangular components and apply the cross product equation.


    A_x \amp= \N{60} \amp A_y \amp= \N{0}


    B_x \amp= \N{40}\cos \ang{45} \amp B_y \amp= -\N{40} \sin \ang{45}


    \amp = \N{28.28} \amp \amp= \N{-28.28}



    \vec{A} \times \vec{B} \amp = (A_x B_y - A_y B_x) \khat


    \amp = \N{(60)(-28.28) - (0)(28.28)}^2 \khat


    \amp = \N{-1697}^2 \; \khat

Given:


    \vec{A} \amp = \m{\langle 2,4,-1 \rangle}


    \vec{B} \amp = \N{\langle 10, 25, 20 \rangle}


.

li.p:


    P \cos \theta \amp = \N{48.8} \amp P \sin \theta \amp = \N{69.0}


    P\amp = \N{84.5} \amp \theta \amp = \ang{54.7}


.

solution.p:
After evaluating, we learn that


    A \amp = \N{196.4} \amp B \amp = \N{192.2} \amp C \amp = \N{98.5}


.

li.p:
Use trigonometry to find the perpendicular distances.


    d_1 = 40 \cos \alpha = \ft{35.8}


    d_2 = 40 \sin \theta = \ft{13.06}

Apply the definition of the moment , and solve for the moments.


    M_1 \amp = W d_1 = (\lb{500})(\ft{35.8}) =\ftlb{17,885}


    M_2 \amp = T d_2 = (\lb{1500})(\ft{13.06}) = \ftlb{19,590}

Use the law of sines and law of cosines to find distance d from A to C and the angle \phi in triangle ABC.


    d^2 \amp= 5.5^2 + 3^2 - 2(3)(5.5) \cos \ang{120}


    d \amp = \m{7.47}



    \frac{d}{\sin \ang{120}} \amp = \frac{\m{5.5}}{\sin \phi} \implies \phi = \ang{39.6}

Then, find the angle \theta in the right triangle and solve for the perpendicular distance.


    \theta \amp= \ang{180} - \ang{30} - \ang{50}- \phi = \ang{60.36}


    d_\perp \amp = d\ \sin \theta = \m{6.49}

statement.p:
            For each scalar component, determine whether the moment is clockwise or counterclockwise, and apply the right-hand rule to determine the direction of the corresponding moment vector.




M_1 = \Nm{30}




M_2 = \kNm{-400}




M_3 = \Nm{25} \circlearrowright




M_4 = \ftlb{-100} \circlearrowright

solution.p:
Manually attaching the positive or negative signs according to the right-hand rule turns the magnitudes into scalar moments:


    M_1 = \kNm{-25}


    M_2 =\kNm{+40}


    M_3 = \kNm{-30}

Force \vec{F} acts \ang{60} from the vertical with a \lb{750} magnitude, so its horizontal and vertical components are


    F_x \amp = F \sin \ang{60} = \lb{649.5}


    F_y \amp = F \cos \ang{60} = \lb{375.0}

Find the components of \vec{r} and \vec{F}.


    \vec{F} \amp = \lbf{500} \langle \cos \ang{56.3}, \sin \ang{56.3} \rangle


    \amp = \lbf{\langle 277.35, 416.025 \rangle}


    \vec{r} \amp = \ft{\langle 6,3 \rangle}

li.p:
Use right triangle trigonometry to find d_{\perp}.


    \sin \theta \amp = \frac{d_{\perp}}{AC}


    d_{\perp} \amp = AC\left(\sin \theta\right)


    \amp = 4\left(\sin \ang{56.3}\right)


    \amp = \ft{3.328}

Finally, compute the moment about A.


    M_A \amp=F\ d_{\perp}


    \amp =\ft{3.328}\left(\lbf{500}\right)


    \amp = \ftlbf{1664.10}


    \vec{M}_A \amp =\ftlbf{1664.10}\left(+\khat\right)


The \left(+\khat\right) direction of \vec{M}_A comes from the observation of the right-hand rule, as scalar moment computations are not directional.

subsection.p:
The mathematics of cross products was discussed in , and equation provides one method to calculate a moment cross products
\vec{M} \amp = |\vec{r}| |\vec{F}| \sin \theta \, \hat{\vec{u}}
. Here, \theta is the angle between the two vectors as shown in above, and \hat{\vec{u}} is the unit vector perpendicular to both \vec{r} and \vec{F} with the direction coming from the right-hand rule. This equation is useful if you know or can find the magnitudes of \vec{r} and \vec{F} and the angle \theta between them. This equation is the vector equivalent of .

Alternatively, if you know or can find the components of the position \vec{r} and force \vec{F} vectors, it's typically easiest to evaluate the moment cross product using the determinant form discussed in .


    \vec{M} \amp = \vec{r} \times \vec{F}


    \amp= \begin{vmatrix} \ihat \amp \jhat \amp \khat \\ r_x \amp r_y \amp r_z \\ F_x \amp F_y \amp F_z \end{vmatrix}


    \amp = (r_y F_z - r_z F_y)\ \ihat - (r_x F_z - r_z F_x)\ \jhat + (r_x F_y - r_y F_x)\ \khat

In two dimensions, r_z and F_z are zero, so  reduces to


    \vec{M} \amp = \vec{r} \times \vec{F}


    \amp= \begin{vmatrix} \ihat \amp \jhat \amp \khat \\ r_x \amp r_y \amp 0 \\ F_x \amp F_y \amp 0 \end{vmatrix}


    \amp = ( {r_x}{F_y} - {r_y}{F_x})\ \khat


.
          This is just the vector equivalent of Varignon's Theorem in two dimensions, with the correct signs automatically determined from the signs on the scalar components of \vec{F} and \vec{r}.

li.p:
Find the position vector \vec{BD}: Find position vectors by either subtracting the start-point coordinates from the end-point coordinates or focusing on the changes in the position components from B to D.


    \vec{BD} \amp = D-B


    \amp= \m{(0.4, 0, 1.0)} -  \m{(-0.9, 1.1, 0)}


    \amp = \m{\left\langle 1.3, -1.1, 1 \right\rangle}

Find the unit vector of \vec{BD}: Compute a unit vector by dividing \vec{BD} by the total length of BD.


    BD \amp = |\vec{BD}|=\sqrt{1.3^2+(-1.1)^2+1.0^2}


    \amp = \m{1.975}


    \vec{\widehat{BD}} \amp = \frac{\vec{BD}}{BD}


    \amp = \frac {\m{\left\langle 1.3, -1.1, 1 \right\rangle}}{\m{1.975}}


    \amp = \left\langle 0.658, -0.557, 0.506 \right\rangle


Note that \vec{\widehat{BD}} is unitless and is the pure direction of \vec{BD}.

Moment arm \vec{r}_{OB} starts at the point we are taking the moment around, O, and ends at the point B.


    \vec{OB}\amp =B-O


    \amp =\m{(-0.9, 1.1, 0)} - \m{(0, 0, 0)}


    \amp = \m{\left\langle -0.9, 1.1, 0 \right\rangle}

Cross \vec{r}_{OB} with \vec{F}_{BD} to find the moment of \vec{F}_{BD} about point O.


    \vec{M}_O \amp = \vec{r}_{OB} \times \vec{F}_{BD}


    \amp = \begin{vmatrix} \ihat \amp \jhat \amp \khat \\ -0.9 \amp 1.1 \amp 0 \\ 1.317 \amp -1.114 \amp 1.013 \end{vmatrix}


    \amp = \kN{\left\langle 1.114, 0.911, -0.446 \right\rangle}

Moment arm \vec{r}_{OD} starts at the point we are taking the moment around, O, and ends at the point D.


    \vec{OD}\amp=D-O


    \amp =\m{(0.4, 0, 1.0)} - \m{(0, 0, 0)}


    \amp = \m{\left\langle 0.4, 0, 1.0 \right\rangle}

Cross \vec{r}_{OD} with \vec{F}_{BD} to find the moment of \vec{F}_{BD} about point O.


    \vec{M_O} \amp = \vec{r}_{OD} \times \vec{F}_{BD}


    \amp = \begin{vmatrix} \ihat \amp \jhat \amp \khat \\ 0.4 \amp 0 \amp 1.0\\ 1.317 \amp -1.114 \amp 1.013 \end{vmatrix}


    \amp = \kN{\left\langle 1.114, 0.911, -0.446 \right\rangle}

subsection.p:
        Two-Step Method: Separates the cross and dot products into two distinct steps.


First, compute the moment of a the force about any point on the line of interest using the cross product, . \vec{M} = (\vec{r} \times \vec{F})

Next, find the scalar projection of the moment onto the line of interest using a dot product, . M_{line}=\|\proj_{\vec{u}} \vec{M}\| = \vec{M} \cdot \hat{\vec{u}}

solution.p:
              Option 1: The Two-Step Method separates the cross and dot products into two distinct steps.


Compute the vector moment of \vec{P_1} about any point on line \vec{BG}, including \vec{M_G} or \vec{M_B}, then

Dot \vec{M} onto the unit vector \widehat{BG}

Next, the middle row of the scalar triple product is one of the moment arms from \vec{BG} to the force \vec{P_2}.
          There are four options that will all result in the same answer after you compute the scalar triple product.


    \vec{r}_{GH} \amp= \ft{\langle 0.0, -2.0, 0.0 \rangle}


    \vec{r}_{GD} \amp= \ft{\langle 2.0, -2.0, 0.0 \rangle}


    \vec{r}_{BH} \amp= \ft{\langle -2.0, -2.0, 2.0 \rangle}


    \vec{r}_{BD} \amp= \ft{\langle 0.0, -2.0, 2.0 \rangle}



    M \amp = P d


    \amp = (\lb{1200} )(\inch{9})


    \amp = \inlb{10800}


    \amp = \ftlb{900}


.

section.p:
These are vector equations; hidden within each are three independent scalar equations, one for each coordinate direction.


    \sum\vec{F} \amp= 0 \implies \begin{cases}\Sigma F_x \amp= 0\\ \Sigma F_y \amp= 0 \\ \Sigma F_z \amp= 0\end{cases} \amp \sum\vec{M} \amp= 0 \implies \begin{cases}\Sigma M_x \amp= 0\\ \Sigma M_y \amp= 0 \\ \Sigma M_z \amp= 0\end{cases}


Working with these scalar equations is often easier than using their vector equivalents, particularly in two-dimensional problems.

Two-dimensional rigid bodies have three degrees of freedom, so they only require three independent equilibrium equations to solve. The six scalar equations of can easily be reduced to three by eliminating the equations which refer to the unused z dimension. For objects in the xy plane there are no forces acting in the z direction to create moments about the x or y axes, so the reduced set of three equations is
\{1\} \amp = \begin{cases} \sum F_x \amp =0 \\ \sum F_y \amp =0 \\ \sum M_A \amp =0 \end{cases}
where the subscript z has been replaced with a letter to indicate an arbitrary moment center in the xy plane instead of a perpendicular z axis.

This is not the only possible set of equilibrium equations. Either force equation can be replaced with a linearly independent moment equation about a point of your choosing Labels A, B and C in these equations are representative. They don't have to correspond to points A, B and C on your problem., so the other possible sets are
\{2\} \amp = \begin{cases} \sum F_x \amp =0 \\ \sum M_B \amp =0 \\ \sum M_A \amp =0 \end{cases} \amp \{3\} \amp = \begin{cases} \sum M_C \amp =0 \\ \sum F_y \amp =0 \\ \sum M_A \amp =0 \end{cases} \amp \{4\} \amp = \begin{cases} \sum M_C \amp =0 \\ \sum M_B \amp =0 \\ \sum M_A \amp =0 \end{cases}
For set four, moment centers A, B, and C must form a triangle to ensure the three equations are linearly independent.

solution.p:
The magnitude of force B is unknown but its direction is known, so the x and y components of B can be expressed as


    B_x \amp= B \sin \ang{60} \amp B_y \amp = B \cos \ang{60}


.

Of these three equations only the third can be evaluated immediately, because we know C and D.
In equations (1) and (2) unknowns A_x and A_y can't be found until B is known.
Inserting the known values into (3) and solving for B gives


    B \amp = \frac{4 (250) + 500 }{6.098}


    \amp = \frac{\ftlb{1500}}{\ft{6.098}}


    \amp = \lb{246.0}

Now with the magnitude of B known, A_x and A_y can be found with (1) and (2).


    A_x \amp = B \sin \ang{60}


    \amp = 246.0 \sin \ang{60}


    \amp = \lb{213.0}



    A_y \amp= C - B \cos \ang{60}


    \amp= 250 - 246.0 \cos \ang{60}


    \amp= \lb{127.0}


The positive signs on these values indicate that the directions assumed on the free-body diagram were correct.

Substituting B and C into equation (1) yields


    A_{x'} \amp = -B + C \sin \ang{30}


    \amp = - 246.0 + 250 \sin \ang{30}


    \amp = -\lb{121.0}

li.p:
The force of the cable acts from A to B.
This direction is described by the displacement vector from A to B\vec{r}_{AB} = \ft{(2 \ihat- 4 \jhat - 1 \khat )} or the corresponding unit vector


    \lambda_{AB} \amp = \frac{\vec{r}_{AB}}{\left|\vec{r}_{AB}\right|}


    \amp = \frac{2 \ihat- 4 \jhat - 1 \khat}{\sqrt{(2)^2 + (-4)^2 + (-1)^2 }}


    \amp = \frac{2 \ihat- 4 \jhat - 1 \khat}{\sqrt{21}}.

Multiplying the unit vector by the cable tension gives the force acting on A as a three-dimensional Cartesian force vector.


    \vec{F} \amp = \lambda_{AB} T


    \amp = \left( \frac{2 \ihat - 4\jhat- 1 \khat}{\sqrt{21}} \right) \lb{500}


    \amp = \left( 2 \ihat - 4\jhat- 1 \khat \right) \lb{\left(\frac{500}{\sqrt{21}}\right)}


\vec{F} = \lb{(218 \ihat - 436 \jhat - 109 \khat)}

The resulting vector equations for the reaction force \vec{C} and reaction moment \vec{M} are


    \vec{C} \amp= \lb{(-218 \ihat + 436 \jhat + 109 \khat)}


    \vec{M} \amp = \ftlb{(-2182 \ihat -1528 \jhat + 1746 \khat)}.

solution.p:
Find final exam score FE so that your average grade \bar{G} is 70%.


    \bar{G} \amp= \frac{\sum G_i w_i}{\sum w_i}


    70 \amp = \frac{(40 \times 0.25) + (80 \times 0.25) + (90 \times 0.1) + (FE \times 0.4)}{(0.25 + 0.25 + 0.1 + 0.4 )}


    FE \amp = \frac{70 (1) - (10 +20 + 9)}{0.4} = 77.5


.

section.p:
The moment of total weight W is also clockwise around point O, so the sign of moment will also be negative according to the right-hand rule. Since the two representation are equivalent we can equate them and solve for \bar{x}.
-\bar{x} W \amp=-x_1 W_1 -x_2 W_2
\bar{x} \amp =\frac{x_1 W_1 +x_2 W_2}{W_1 + W_2}

solution.p:
Let x_i represent the x-coordinates of the individual weights, and \bar{x} represent the x-coordinate of the center of gravity.
For both systems to be equivalent:


    W\ \bar{x} \amp= \sum (W_i\ x_i)


    (\N{90})\ \bar{x} \amp = (\N{50})(0) + (\N{10})(\m{1.2}) + (\N{30})(\m{2.0})


    \bar{x} \amp= \frac{\Nm{72}}{\N{90}}


    \amp= \m{0.8}

The cross section is symmetrical about both a vertical and horizontal centerline.
            The centroid is at the intersection, in the middle.
            The coordinates are measured from the origin, in the bottom left of the diagram. \bar{x} = \bar{y} = \cm{3.5}

subsection.p:
For a homogeneous flat plate with uniform thickness, like a piece of plywood, the density, thickness and g are all constant so


    \amp \amp W_i \amp = \rho g t A_i



    \bar{x}\amp = \frac{\sum \bar{x}_{i} W_i}{\sum W_i} \amp \bar{y}\amp = \frac{\sum \bar{y}_{i} W_i}{\sum W_i} \amp \bar{z}\amp = \frac{\sum \bar{z}_{i} W_i}{\sum W_i}


    \bar{x}\amp = \frac{ \cancel{\rho g t} \sum \bar{x}_{i} A_i}{\cancel{\rho g t}\sum A_i} \amp \bar{y}\amp = \frac{\cancel{\rho g t}\sum \bar{y}_{i} A_i}{\cancel{\rho g t}\sum A_i} \amp \bar{z}\amp = \frac{\cancel{\rho g t}\sum \bar{z}_{i} A_i}{\cancel{\rho g t}\sum A_i}


.

The centroid of the whole shape is found by applying 


    \bar{x} \amp = \frac{ \sum {\bar{x}_i}\ A_i}{\sum A_i} \amp \bar{y} \amp = \frac{ \sum {\bar{y}_i}\ A_i}{\sum A_i}


    \bar{x} \amp = \frac{ (1.5)(18)+(3.5) (14)}{18 + 14} \amp \bar{y} \amp = \frac{ \sum (5)(18)+(1) (14)}{18 + 14}


    \bar{x} \amp = \inch{2.375} \amp\bar{y} \amp = \inch{3.25}

li.p:
After you have filled in the whole table, you can find the coordinates of the centroid by applying  with the summary values from the last row.


    \bar{x} \amp = \frac{Q_y}{A} \amp = \frac{112.8}{24.93} \amp = \cm{4.52}


    \bar{y} \amp = \frac{Q_x}{A} \amp = \frac{41.86}{24.93} \amp = \cm{1.692}

subsection.p:
The centroid of a three-dimensional volume is found similarly to two-dimensional centroids, but with volume used instead of area for the weighting factor.
The centroid of a volume and the center of mass or gravity for a homogenous solid are identical.


    \bar{x}\amp = \frac{\sum \bar{x}_i\ V_i }{\sum V_i}\amp \bar{y}\amp = \frac{\sum \bar{y}_i\ V_i }{\sum V_i}\amp \bar{z}\amp = \frac{\sum \bar{z}_i\ V_i }{\sum V_i}

If the density varies for each part of a composite solid, we can find the center of mass by dividing the first moment of mass by the total mass. You can also compute the center of gravity by replacing the mass terms in the equations below with weight terms.
\bar{x}\amp = \frac{\sum \bar{x}_i\ m_i }{\sum m_i} \amp \bar{y}\amp = \frac{\sum \bar{y}_i\ m_i }{\sum m_i}\amp \bar{z}\amp = \frac{\sum \bar{z}_i\ m_i }{\sum m_i}
Here m_i is the mass of composite part i.

section.p:
This approach is true for any choice of weighting function.
To find \bar{x} for a two dimensional area, the value to be averaged is x and the weighting function is dA, so replacing dx with dA and x_i with x,


    \bar{x}\amp = \frac{\sum \bar{x}_{i} A_i}{\sum A_i} \amp \amp \Rightarrow \amp \bar{x}\amp = \frac{\int x dA}{\int dA}

The two-dimensional centroid equations  become,


    \bar{x} \amp= \frac{ \sum {\bar{x}_i}\ A_i}{\sum A_i} \Rightarrow \frac{ \int \bar{x}_{\text{el}}\ dA}{\int dA} \amp \bar{y} \amp = \frac{ \sum {\bar{y}_i}\ A_i}{\sum A_i} \Rightarrow \frac{ \int \bar{y}_{\text{el}}\ dA}{\int dA}


,
and in the same way the center of gravity equations become


    \bar{x} \amp = \frac{ \int \bar{x}_{\text{el}}\ dW}{\int dW} \amp \bar{y} \amp = \frac{ \int \bar{y}_{\text{el}}\ dW}{\int dW} \amp \bar{z} \amp = \frac{ \int \bar{z}_{\text{el}}\ dW}{\int dW}


.

subsection.p:
With these details established, the next step is to set up and evaluate the integral A = \int dA = \int_0^a y\ dx.
This is the familiar formula from calculus for the area under a curve.
Proceeding with the integration


    A \amp = \int dA \amp \left(dA = y\ dx\right)


     \amp = \int_0^a y\ dx \amp \left(y = kx^n\right)


    \amp = \int_0^a k x^n dx \amp \text{(integrate)}


    \amp = k \left.
  \frac{x^{n+1}}{n+1} \right \vert_0^a \amp \text{(evaluate limits)}


    \amp = k \frac{a^{n+1}}{n+1} \amp \left(k = \frac{b}{a^n}\right)


    \amp = \frac{b}{a^n} \frac{a^{n+1}}{n+1} \amp \text{(simplify)}


    A \amp = \frac{ab}{n+1} \amp \text{(result)}

li.p:
Substituting the results into the definitions gives


    \bar{x} \amp = \frac{Q_y}{A} \amp \bar{y} \amp = \frac{Q_x}{A}


    \amp = \frac{b^2h}{2} \bigg/ { bh} \amp \amp = \frac{h^2b}{2} \bigg/ { bh}


    \amp = \frac{b}{2}\amp \amp = \frac{h}{2}


.

Substituting the results into the definitions gives


    \bar{x} \amp = \frac{Q_y}{A} \amp \bar{y} \amp = \frac{Q_x}{A}


    \amp = \frac{b^2h}{2} \bigg/ { bh} \amp \amp = \frac{h^2b}{2} \bigg/ { bh}


    \amp = \frac{b}{2}\amp \amp = \frac{h}{2}


.

Substituting the results into the definitions gives


    \bar{x} \amp = \frac{Q_y}{A} \amp \bar{y} \amp = \frac{Q_x}{A}


    \amp = \frac{b^2h}{2} \bigg/ { bh} \amp \amp = \frac{h^2b}{2} \bigg/ { bh}


    \amp = \frac{b}{2}\amp \amp = \frac{h}{2}


.

Substituting the results into the definitions gives


    \bar{x} \amp = \frac{Q_y}{A} \amp \bar{y} \amp = \frac{Q_x}{A}


    \amp = \frac{b^2h}{3} \bigg/ \frac{bh}{2} \amp \amp = \frac{h^2b}{6} \bigg/ \frac{bh}{2}


    \amp = \frac{2}{3}b\amp \amp = \frac{1}{3}h


.

Substituting the results into the definitions gives


    \bar{x} \amp = \frac{Q_y}{A} \amp \bar{y} \amp = \frac{Q_x}{A}


    \amp = \frac{b^2h}{3} \bigg/ \frac{bh}{2} \amp \amp = \frac{h^2b}{6} \bigg/ \frac{bh}{2}


    \amp = \frac{2}{3}b\amp \amp = \frac{1}{3}h


.

Substituting Q_x and Q_y along with A = bh/2 into the centroid definitions gives


    \bar{x} \amp = \frac{Q_y}{A} \amp \bar{y} \amp = \frac{Q_x}{A}


    \amp = \frac{b^2h}{3} \bigg/ \frac{bh}{2} \amp \amp = \frac{h^2b}{6} \bigg/ \frac{bh}{2}


    \amp = \frac{2}{3}b\amp \amp = \frac{1}{3}h


.

Substituting the results into the definitions gives


    \bar{x} \amp = \frac{Q_y}{A} \amp \bar{y} \amp = \frac{Q_x}{A}


    \amp = \frac{ba^2}{4 } \bigg/ \frac{2 ba}{3} \amp \amp = \frac{2 b^2a }{5}\bigg/ \frac{2 ba}{3}


    \amp = \frac{3}{8} a \amp \amp = \frac{3}{5} b

Substituting the results into the definitions gives


    \bar{x} \amp = \frac{Q_y}{A} \amp \bar{y} \amp = \frac{Q_x}{A}


    \amp = \frac{1}{384} \bigg/ \frac{1}{96} \amp \amp = \frac{1}{1920} \bigg/ \frac{1}{96}


    \bar{x} \amp= \frac{1}{4} \amp \bar{y}\amp =\frac{1}{20}


.

Substituting the results into the definitions gives


    \bar{x} \amp = \frac{Q_y}{A} \amp \bar{y} \amp = \frac{Q_x}{A}


    \amp = \frac{1}{384} \bigg/ \frac{1}{96} \amp \amp = \frac{1}{1920} \bigg/ \frac{1}{96}


    \bar{x} \amp= \frac{1}{4} \amp \bar{y}\amp =\frac{1}{20}


.

Substituting the results into the definitions gives


    \bar{y} \amp = \frac{Q_x}{A}


    \amp = \frac{2 r^3}{3} \bigg/ \frac{\pi r^2}{2}


    \amp = \frac{4r}{3\pi}


.

subsection.p:
The magnitude of the distributed load of the books is the total weight of the books divided by the length of the shelf w(x) = \frac{\Sigma W_i}{\ell}. It represents the average book weight per unit length. Similarly, the total weight of the books is equal to the value of the distributed load times the length of the shelf or
W \amp = w(x) \ell
\text{total weight} \amp = \frac{\text{weight}}{\text{length}} \times\ \text{length of shelf}

solution.p:
Then apply the equations of equilibrium.


    \sum M_A \amp = 0


    +(\lbperin{12})(\inch{10}) (\inch{5}) -(\lb{100}) (\inch{6})


    -(\lb{150})(\inch{12}) -(\lb{100}) ( \inch{18})


    +(B_y) (\inch{18}) - (\lbperin{12}) (\inch{10}) (\inch{29})\amp = 0 \rightarrow \amp B_y \amp= \lb{393.3}



    \sum F_y\amp = 0


    -(\lbperin{12}) (\inch{10}) + A_y - \lb{100} - \lb{150}


    - \lb{100} +B_y - (\lbperin{12})( \inch{10})\amp = 0 \rightarrow \amp A_y\amp= \lb{196.7}



    \sum F_x \amp = 0 \rightarrow \amp A_x \amp = 0

The pressure at the top and the bottom of the window are


    P_A \amp = \rho\ g (\m{2})= \Nsm{19620}


    P_B \amp = \rho\ g (\m{5}) = \Nsm{49050}

Since the loading is linear, the average pressure acting on the window is


    P_{ave}\amp = (P_A + P_B)/2


    \amp = \Nsm{34300}

The total force acting on the window is the average pressure times the area of the window


    F \amp = (P_{ave})( \m{3} \times \m{1.5})


    \amp = \kN{155}

Dividing the trapezoid into a triangle and a rectangle and measuring down from the surface of the tank, the distance to the equivalent force is


    d \amp= \frac{\sum A_i \bar{y}_i}{\sum A_i}


    d \amp = \frac{\big[P_A(\m{3})\big](\m{3.5}) + \left[\dfrac{1}{2}(P_B-P_A)(\m{3})\right](\m{4})} { \big[P_A(\m{3})\big] +\left[\dfrac{1}{2}(P_B-P_A)(\m{3})\right]}


    d \amp = \m{3.71}


If you prefer, you may use the formula from the Centroid table to locate the centroid of the trapezoid instead.

For the gate to tip, the equivalent force of the water must act at or above A.
That happens when the centroid of the load intensity diagram of the water is at or above A, so


    \frac{d}{3} \amp \ge \mm{500}


    d \amp \ge \mm{1500}


.

li.p:
Calculate the areas.  Notice that the areas have moment units.


    A_1 \amp = \ft{4} \times \lb{65} \amp A_4 \amp = \dfrac{ \ft{6.5} \times \lb{-65}}{2} 

  \amp = \ftlb{260}\amp \amp = \ftlb{-211.25}



    A_2 \amp = \ft{2} \times \lb{35} \amp A_5 \amp = \ft{4} \times \lb{-65}


    \amp = \ftlb{70}\amp \amp  = \ftlb{-260}




    A_3 \amp = \dfrac{ \ft{3.5} \times \lb{35}}{2} \amp A_6 \amp = \ft{4} \times \lb{20}


    \amp = \ftlb{61.25} \amp \amp = \ftlb{80}

The constants are evaluated by matching the functions at the boundary x=\m{10}, where


    V_1(10) = -\kN{45}


    M_1(10) = \kNm{150}


Evaluating V_2(x') and M_2(x') at x' = 0 we find that


    C_2 \amp= -\kN{45}


    C_3 \amp= \kNm{150}

subsection.p:
Recall that a resultants is the sum of two or more vectors.
        The friction resultant is the vector sum of the friction and normal forces.
        Since these two forces are perpendicular, the magnitude of the friction resultant can be found using the Pythagorean theorem. R = \sqrt{F_f^2 + N^2}

solution.p:
Since the wedge is at the point of impending motion,


    F_2 \amp= \mu_s N_2


    \amp= \lb{2.43}

Apply  with \mu = 0.23 and \beta = 4.5 \times (2 \pi) = 28.27~\text{radians}.

            \dfrac{T_+}{T_-} \amp= e^{\mu \beta}
            T_- \amp = \frac{\lb{100}}{e^{0.23 \times 28.27}}
            \amp = \lb{0.15}

subsubsection.p:
If you are not familiar with double integration, briefly you can think of a double integral as two normal single integrals, one inside and the other outside, which are evaluated one at a time from the inside out. Our integral becomes


    I_x \amp = \int_A y^2 dA


    \amp = \iint y^2 \underbrace{dx\ dy}_{dA}


    \amp = \underbrace{\int_\text{bottom}^\text{top} \underbrace{\left [ \int_\text{left}^\text{right} y^2 dx \right ]}_\text{inside} dy }_\text{outside}

subsection.p:
Finding I_x using horizontal strips is anything but easy. In fact, the integral that needs to be solved is this monstrosity


    I_x \amp = \int_A y^2\ (1-x)\ dy


    \amp = \int_0^2 y^2 \left (1- \frac{\sqrt[3]{2} \left ( \sqrt{81 y^2 + 12} + 9y \right )^{2/3} - 2 \sqrt[3]{3}}{6^{2/3} \sqrt[3]{\sqrt{81 y^2 + 12} + 9y}} \right )\ dy


    \amp \dots \text{ and then a miracle occurs}


    I_x \amp = \frac{49}{120}


.

sidebyside.p:


    J_O \amp = I_x + I_y


    \bar{I}_x \amp = \bar{I}_y = \frac{J_O}{2} = \frac{\pi r^4}{4}


.

solution.p:
Looking for \bar{I}_{x'}:


    I \amp = \bar{I} + A d^2 \amp \amp \text{Parallel Axis Theorem, general statement}


    \bar{I}_{x'} \amp = I_x - A (\bar{y})^2 \amp \amp \text{Parallel Axis Theorem, backwards}


    \amp = \frac{bh^3}{12} - \frac{bh}{2} \left( \frac{h}{3} \right )^2 \amp \amp \text{Insert values for triangle}


    \amp = b h^3 \left[ \frac{1}{12} - \frac{1}{18} \right ]\amp \amp \text{Factor out common terms}


    \amp = b h^3 \left[ \frac{3}{36} - \frac{2}{36} \right ]\amp \amp \text{Common denominator}


    \bar{I}_{x'} \amp = \frac{b h^3}{36} \amp \amp \text{Result}

In case 1 the centroids of all three rectangles are on the x axis, so the parallel axis theorem is unnecessary.


    (I_x)_1 \amp = \sum_{i= 1}^3 \bar{I} + A d^2


    \amp = 3 \frac{bh^3}{12}


    \amp = \frac{(1.5)(5.5)^3}{4}


    (I_x)_1 \amp = \inch{62.4}^4

li.p:
Find the distance to the neutral axis


    \bar{y} \amp = \frac{\sum A_i \bar{y_i}}{\sum A_i} = \frac{ 2 A_\text{L} \bar{y}_\text{L} + A_\text{R} \bar{y}_\text{R} } {2 A_\text{L} + A_\text{R}}


    \amp = \frac{ 2 (4.75)(1.98) + (8)(-0.5)} { 2(4.75) + 8}


    \bar{y} \amp = \inch{0.846}


.

The distance between the neutral axis is and the centroids of the subparts are


    d_\text{R} \amp = | \bar{y} - \bar{y}_\text{R} | = | 0.846 - (-0.5)| = \inch{1.346}


    d_\text{L} \amp = | \bar{y} - \bar{y}_\text{L} | = | 0.846 - 1.98| = \inch{1.134}


.

section.p:
The radius of gyration with respect to the x and y axes and the origin are given by these formulas


    k_x \amp = \sqrt{\frac{I_x}{A}} \amp k_y \amp = \sqrt{\frac{I_y}{A}} \amp k_o \amp = \sqrt{\frac{J_o}{A}}


.
